Playwright, Selenium, and Puppeteer are widely used[browser automation tools](https://browser-use.com/posts/browser-automation-tools) . They can be used for end-to-end testing, web scraping, screenshot generation, and increasingly to enable AI agents to use the browser.


In this article, we'll compare their browser support, languages, waiting behavior, and testing tools. We'll also look at when an AI browser agent such as Browser Use fits the task.


## TL;DR


Choose Playwright for a new end-to-end test suite, especially if you want JavaScript or TypeScript tests with integrated debugging tools. Choose Selenium when you need actual Safari testing, an existing Grid setup, or compatibility with your team's established test suite. Puppeteer is a good fit for Node.js scripts that generate PDFs, take screenshots, or scrape pages.


Here's how the three tools compare:


Criterion Playwright Selenium Puppeteer


Best fit New end-to-end test suites Existing test suites, Grid, actual Safari Node.js scraping, screenshots, PDFs


Browsers Chromium, Chrome, Edge; patched Firefox and WebKit Chrome, Edge, Firefox, Safari Chrome and Firefox; no WebKit


Languages JavaScript, TypeScript, Python, Java, .NET Java, Python, C#, JavaScript, Ruby JavaScript, TypeScript


Protocol CDP for Chromium; custom protocols for Firefox and WebKit WebDriver Classic and WebDriver BiDi CDP by default for Chrome; BiDi for Firefox


Waiting Actionability checks on locator actions; retrying assertions in Playwright Test Explicit waits for application conditions Locator actions auto-wait; lower-level APIs need explicit handling


Test runner Playwright Test for JavaScript/TypeScript; language-specific integrations elsewhere Pair WebDriver with a test runner Pair the library with a test runner


Parallel execution Playwright Test workers and CI sharding Test runner parallelism and Selenium Grid Build concurrency into your script or runner


Debugging Inspector, Trace Viewer, Playwright Test reports Browser/driver logs and test runner tooling Chrome DevTools and CDP access


Setup Install package and browser builds Selenium Manager handles supported drivers automatically Install package and supported browsers


Main limitation WebKit is not the Safari app; Firefox requires a patched build More synchronization and test tooling to assemble No WebKit; CDP-specific features don't transfer to Firefox


The browser distinction matters: Playwright covers three rendering engines, while Selenium can drive Apple's Safari application. Puppeteer supports Firefox as well as Chrome, so browser choice alone no longer separates it from Playwright as clearly as it once did.


## Selenium


[Selenium](https://www.selenium.dev/) is a pioneering framework for browser automation. It was started by Jason Huggins in 2004. Over time, the team behind Selenium developed multiple browser automation tools, including:


- Selenium IDE (2006) -- a browser extension that lets you record, edit, and play back interactions with websites and web apps.
- Selenium Grid (2008) -- allows running web tests in parallel across multiple machines, browsers, and operating systems at the same time.
- Selenium WebDriver (2011) -- formed the basis for the[W3C WebDriver standard](https://www.w3.org/TR/webdriver1/) , which became a W3C Recommendation in 2018. Most major web browsers support it, including Chrome, Firefox, Safari, and Edge.


